1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,930

2
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:07,990
Today for our talk, we have
Professor Jake Williams

3
00:00:07,990 --> 00:00:09,830
from Drexel University.

4
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:13,390
He is an associate professor
at information science

5
00:00:13,390 --> 00:00:17,020
at Drexel University's College
of Computing and Informatics

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,120
in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.

7
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:22,270
Dr. Williams has a background
in physics and math

8
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:24,760
with degrees from the
University of Vermont,

9
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:27,820
and his research leverages
a quantitative linguistics

10
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:32,560
perspective that applies math
and statistical methodologies

11
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:36,250
to analyze and improve
linguistic learning systems.

12
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:39,340
Following a one-year postdoc
appointment at the University

13
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:42,370
of Berkeley, studying
large language,

14
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:45,550
large-scale machine
learning in 2015,

15
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:49,000
Dr. Williams became a data
science faculty at Drexel

16
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,170
where he drove the foundation
of a DSMS program and develops

17
00:00:53,170 --> 00:00:57,370
and instruct data
science coursework,

18
00:00:57,370 --> 00:01:00,460
including natural language
processing with deep learning.

19
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:04,879
So welcome, and thank you for
coming today for your talk.

20
00:01:04,879 --> 00:01:07,430
And you could do a quick
introduction of yourself

21
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:08,430
before you start.

22
00:01:08,430 --> 00:01:08,930
Great.

23
00:01:08,930 --> 00:01:09,555
Thanks so much.

24
00:01:09,555 --> 00:01:11,330
I got the mic here.

25
00:01:11,330 --> 00:01:12,630
Nice to see you all here.

26
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:16,080
Thanks for coming out, and
also for showing up online.

27
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,670
It's a pleasure to be here.

28
00:01:19,670 --> 00:01:21,890
As was mentioned,
my name is Jake,

29
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:24,330
and my background is
in math and physics.

30
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:27,217
So the perspective that I'm
coming from towards this work

31
00:01:27,217 --> 00:01:29,300
might be a little bit
different than the standard,

32
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:32,810
and that will be a theme
throughout the discussion.

33
00:01:32,810 --> 00:01:35,270
The purpose of
this discussion is

34
00:01:35,270 --> 00:01:40,970
to go through a relatively
long-term development, a project

35
00:01:40,970 --> 00:01:43,730
that I've been working on.

36
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:46,880
And as mentioned, my background
is in quantitative linguistics,

37
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:50,240
which means my history
of focus on language

38
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:56,780
has primarily been to
develop general theories

39
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,660
and descriptions of
phenomena that you observe

40
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:03,830
with regards to linguistic
units, whatever those might be.

41
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:06,080
It's a statistical
approach which,

42
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:10,580
based on theories of
language generation

43
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:13,020
that are statistical in basis.

44
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:18,470
And over the course of
my time as a researcher,

45
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:22,940
I've explored and ventured
into language modeling itself,

46
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:25,940
and ultimately, into neural
networks as they approach

47
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:27,810
language modeling themselves.

48
00:02:27,810 --> 00:02:33,410
And that's what brought
me here through quite

49
00:02:33,410 --> 00:02:34,320
a bit of other work.

50
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,650
So if you look into
my profile, you'll

51
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:39,390
see a lot of different
subjects in either applied NLP,

52
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:42,200
like I said,
quantitative linguistic.

53
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:45,170
And neural networks is a
natural transition for me

54
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:49,490
into inferential work.

55
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:50,605
So let's get started.

56
00:02:50,605 --> 00:02:54,282

57
00:02:54,282 --> 00:02:56,490
Well, this is how we'll
start the conversation today.

58
00:02:56,490 --> 00:02:58,940
It's not exactly how
we got here in my lab.

59
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:03,800
We came at this subject
from a different approach,

60
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:07,660
trying to think about
layer initializations

61
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:09,700
in neural networks.

62
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:12,670
And this subject
that we're discussing

63
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:16,990
as a front for this
talk is specifically

64
00:03:16,990 --> 00:03:19,370
focused on transformer
architecture components,

65
00:03:19,370 --> 00:03:21,640
the self-attention
component that's

66
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:25,030
pivotal to the success of
the transformer architecture.

67
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:29,260
And it focuses on the fact
that self-attention requires

68
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:31,030
a quadratic
comparison of vectors

69
00:03:31,030 --> 00:03:35,080
in order to produce the feature
weights of those vectors needed

70
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:38,140
to model long-range
dependencies in text.

71
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:40,060
Commonly, parameters
for self-attention

72
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:41,920
are based on a
transformation matrix.

73
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:46,690
Two, usually, queries
and keys, that

74
00:03:46,690 --> 00:03:49,400
are responsible for
dimensionalizing input vectors.

75
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:52,450
And I describe it this way
because generally speaking,

76
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:54,610
when you're at the point
of a self-attention layer,

77
00:03:54,610 --> 00:03:56,530
you already have low
dimensional vectors.

78
00:03:56,530 --> 00:04:00,580
But the parameters in a
standard self-attention layer

79
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:03,460
are changing the dimensionality
and the structure

80
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:04,790
of that dimensional space.

81
00:04:04,790 --> 00:04:06,930
They are like an
embedding layer,

82
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:10,280
which is factorizing the
embedding dimensions.

83
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:12,770
This re-dimensionalization
is the primary means

84
00:04:12,770 --> 00:04:15,260
by which self-attention
creates feature weights.

85
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:20,870
It really just computes
similarity in that shared space.

86
00:04:20,870 --> 00:04:23,240
Large and similar inner
products really just

87
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:24,968
result in strongly
weighted features.

88
00:04:24,968 --> 00:04:26,510
So it's up to that
dimensionalization

89
00:04:26,510 --> 00:04:30,410
to produce good similarities
for whatever purpose

90
00:04:30,410 --> 00:04:33,000
your prediction requires.

91
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,210
However, an alternative
strategy for future waits

92
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:37,440
might ask given a basis.

93
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:38,940
So in other words,
you're stuck with

94
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,220
your low dimensional vectors.

95
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:44,460
What is the optimal way to
convert those comparisons

96
00:04:44,460 --> 00:04:48,420
of the vectors you're looking
at by a matrix transformation

97
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:51,030
to modify the vector
similarities that you

98
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:54,690
are stuck with that
correspond to the best

99
00:04:54,690 --> 00:04:55,630
weights for features?

100
00:04:55,630 --> 00:04:58,320
In other words, treat this
as a feedforward layer

101
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:01,410
to produce self-attention
weights as opposed to try

102
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:06,030
and transform to some basis that
produces good feature weights.

103
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:08,820
The use of this modified
self-attention mechanism

104
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:11,405
will be part and parcel
the substance of this talk.

105
00:05:11,405 --> 00:05:15,810

106
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:18,505
It's worth noting that this
alternative mechanism is

107
00:05:18,505 --> 00:05:20,880
entirely compatible with the
traditional dimensionalizing

108
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:22,120
version of self-attention.

109
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:27,240
In other words, you could
still change the dimension

110
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:29,550
and compute
similarities, and then

111
00:05:29,550 --> 00:05:32,310
convert that with a
second feedforward layer

112
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:34,350
to produce optimal
feature weights.

113
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:36,610
This is not
exclusive in any way.

114
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:40,980
This is exploring how useful
that alternative prediction

115
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:43,470
of feature weights can function.

116
00:05:43,470 --> 00:05:45,810
However, we'll avoid
the standard mechanism

117
00:05:45,810 --> 00:05:46,960
for two reasons.

118
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:51,960
First, we have no solution
to the standard parameters

119
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:53,700
for self-attention
as an initialization.

120
00:05:53,700 --> 00:05:58,530
And this will be discussed
at length in slides to come.

121
00:05:58,530 --> 00:06:01,830
Likewise, it would create an
additional model complexity

122
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:03,715
that would muddle the
effects of the modified

123
00:06:03,715 --> 00:06:05,590
form of self-attention
that we wish to study.

124
00:06:05,590 --> 00:06:09,660
So having that
dimensionalization as a way

125
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:12,900
to produce good feature weights
would confuse whether or not

126
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:16,080
the feedforward computation
of feature weights

127
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,845
is functioning well.

128
00:06:17,845 --> 00:06:19,470
There's a catch to
this, however, which

129
00:06:19,470 --> 00:06:22,590
is that these
vectors that we use

130
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:26,790
for such a self-attention
layer better be good.

131
00:06:26,790 --> 00:06:28,470
In other words,
their comparisons

132
00:06:28,470 --> 00:06:33,710
must be consistent and
meaningful in the first place.

133
00:06:33,710 --> 00:06:35,600
So to get it out
of the way, here

134
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:37,820
is an architectural
diagram for the relatively

135
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:43,110
simple near shallow architecture
pattern that we're using.

136
00:06:43,110 --> 00:06:45,038
It doesn't seem like
there are many neurons

137
00:06:45,038 --> 00:06:46,580
in a network of this
type, and that's

138
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:49,370
because all of the
activations are softmax.

139
00:06:49,370 --> 00:06:52,730
Which means despite the fact
that the U-matrix, for example,

140
00:06:52,730 --> 00:06:56,030
is an entire layer,
it's really just going

141
00:06:56,030 --> 00:06:58,100
through a single
prediction non-linearity,

142
00:06:58,100 --> 00:06:59,780
the softmax function.

143
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:01,700
So you can think about
this as essentially

144
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,500
a three-layer
network that might be

145
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:07,070
creating an encoder-decoder
kind of design.

146
00:07:07,070 --> 00:07:09,680
Likewise, the difference
in presentation here over

147
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:13,310
self-attention, which is
parameterized by the matrix W

148
00:07:13,310 --> 00:07:19,970
here, is intending
to show how a--

149
00:07:19,970 --> 00:07:23,150
whether you consider it
the query or the key--

150
00:07:23,150 --> 00:07:27,440
one vector is the pivot
for the comparison that

151
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:29,280
will produce the
feature weights,

152
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:33,480
which is then fed forward
in this model through W.

153
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:36,050
This is the case for
standard self-attention, too.

154
00:07:36,050 --> 00:07:37,500
In other words,
you can reduce it

155
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:40,710
to a biprediction
diagram in this way

156
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:47,130
where a gray vector such as is
depicted here is that pivot.

157
00:07:47,130 --> 00:07:49,110
The attention
distribution coming out

158
00:07:49,110 --> 00:07:51,300
of the W matrix and
the softmax function

159
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:54,450
is indicated by the
vertical red bar

160
00:07:54,450 --> 00:07:57,670
there, which weights the
block of vectors in black.

161
00:07:57,670 --> 00:08:01,560
That includes the
pivot vector in gray,

162
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:04,320
which is then passed through a
feedforward layer often called

163
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:07,140
the values of a standard
self-attention matrix--

164
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:10,170
U.

165
00:08:10,170 --> 00:08:13,200
Since we use U as
a way to reduce

166
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:14,760
the dimensionality
of the prediction

167
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:17,040
that we're trying
to make, we then

168
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,800
feed that forward
through another layer,

169
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:22,030
and then to output.

170
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:25,680
And that's essentially
the relative shallowness

171
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:26,940
that we're talking about here.

172
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:30,000
W is a self-attention
matrix, which

173
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,940
means there's really only
two layers in effect here.

174
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:36,700
And the activation
functions are strange.

175
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:38,159
And you might
wonder, for example,

176
00:08:38,159 --> 00:08:39,840
why we're using a
different activation

177
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:43,289
function to softmax instead
of any of the dimensionally

178
00:08:43,289 --> 00:08:46,590
independent activation functions
like a logistic function

179
00:08:46,590 --> 00:08:48,100
or anything else.

180
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:51,270
And that's because we
have additional insight

181
00:08:51,270 --> 00:08:53,760
into the softmax function
and the parameters that it

182
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:58,290
optimizes, which is very useful.

183
00:08:58,290 --> 00:09:01,050
So let's talk about those
vectors first though,

184
00:09:01,050 --> 00:09:04,750
before we get to
layer initialization.

185
00:09:04,750 --> 00:09:07,380

186
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:10,080
Optimizing the keys and queries
of standard self-attention

187
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:14,220
bears substantial similarity
to token and word embedding.

188
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:17,460
This is because the
key and query matrices

189
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:20,430
have a common dimension
that they project to,

190
00:09:20,430 --> 00:09:23,400
much like you'd see with the
factorization of an embedding

191
00:09:23,400 --> 00:09:24,577
layer on its own.

192
00:09:24,577 --> 00:09:26,285
Think word word2vec,
something like that.

193
00:09:26,285 --> 00:09:29,940

194
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:33,150
Normally, there might be
multiple self-attention heads.

195
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:36,810
And because of the indeterminacy
in creating a different

196
00:09:36,810 --> 00:09:38,460
dimensional space--
in other words,

197
00:09:38,460 --> 00:09:42,630
there are multiple equivalent
reshufflings of those different

198
00:09:42,630 --> 00:09:45,030
dimensions which will
produce the same output--

199
00:09:45,030 --> 00:09:50,010
that indeterminacy is
something that we hypothesize

200
00:09:50,010 --> 00:09:52,680
has bearing on what is now
referred to as the lottery

201
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:53,830
ticket hypothesis.

202
00:09:53,830 --> 00:09:56,480
In other words, that multiple--

203
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:58,230
or this is the way
that I would state it--

204
00:09:58,230 --> 00:10:01,260
but that multiple
different embeddings, which

205
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:04,440
produce different vector spaces,
can be leveraged in parallel

206
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:07,860
to create further robustness
for the model or in the way

207
00:10:07,860 --> 00:10:13,320
that it's implemented that if a
random initialization doesn't do

208
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:15,900
that well, you can eliminate
it from the network,

209
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:18,780
and that subnetwork will do
just as well, even after it's

210
00:10:18,780 --> 00:10:21,330
totally trained.

211
00:10:21,330 --> 00:10:24,360
In other words, having
multiple clones,

212
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:27,120
self-attention heads, which have
no difference in the outputs

213
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:29,280
that they're trying
to predict, is

214
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:31,920
at the root of the
lottery ticket hypothesis.

215
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:34,560
And ultimately, that
invocation of the lottery

216
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:37,672
ticket hypothesis is really a
justification for eliminating

217
00:10:37,672 --> 00:10:39,630
parameters through
substantial cost of training

218
00:10:39,630 --> 00:10:43,920
are essentially wasted as a
result of random parameter

219
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:45,390
initialization.

220
00:10:45,390 --> 00:10:47,370
You might ask
questions like, well,

221
00:10:47,370 --> 00:10:49,480
what is a good initialization?

222
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:52,085
What is a good set of
word embeddings to use?

223
00:10:52,085 --> 00:10:55,410

224
00:10:55,410 --> 00:11:00,132
So how can a lottery
ticket hypothesis interact

225
00:11:00,132 --> 00:11:02,340
with effects of randomly
initialized embedding layers

226
00:11:02,340 --> 00:11:04,680
be avoided when
constructing language

227
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:08,860
models is another question that
is embedded in this discussion.

228
00:11:08,860 --> 00:11:12,460

229
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:15,420
But we shouldn't say that
dimensionality reduction isn't

230
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:15,920
needed.

231
00:11:15,920 --> 00:11:18,970
It's incredibly necessary.

232
00:11:18,970 --> 00:11:21,040
For language modeling,
you absolutely

233
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:22,600
have to work with
reduced dimension

234
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:25,300
unless you're in a
very small vocabulary.

235
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:29,407
For example, like 26 Latin
characters or something

236
00:11:29,407 --> 00:11:30,490
like that, like a wav2vec.

237
00:11:30,490 --> 00:11:33,550

238
00:11:33,550 --> 00:11:36,820
The inherent input dimension
of a large vocabulary model

239
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:39,730
presents many computational
interact abilities

240
00:11:39,730 --> 00:11:42,220
when designing NLP systems,
something that you're probably

241
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:43,870
all very aware of.

242
00:11:43,870 --> 00:11:47,650
Likewise, though, the
distance from embedding layers

243
00:11:47,650 --> 00:11:50,980
to learning information,
the loss at outputs

244
00:11:50,980 --> 00:11:53,990
puts them in a challenging
position to train.

245
00:11:53,990 --> 00:11:56,620
It's really hard to learn
embedding layers because

246
00:11:56,620 --> 00:12:00,040
of the indeterminacy
in the space

247
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:01,460
that you're trying to learn.

248
00:12:01,460 --> 00:12:03,970
You could swap dimensions
and it's equivalent.

249
00:12:03,970 --> 00:12:06,700

250
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:09,890
But the distance means
that they receive

251
00:12:09,890 --> 00:12:13,490
learning information last.

252
00:12:13,490 --> 00:12:16,310
This is a real
challenge, and it's

253
00:12:16,310 --> 00:12:18,050
present in the history
of NLP and deep

254
00:12:18,050 --> 00:12:23,960
learning to vanishing
gradient stuff.

255
00:12:23,960 --> 00:12:26,090
And this is
exacerbated in the way

256
00:12:26,090 --> 00:12:27,800
that we have to
actually learn embedding

257
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:31,490
layers in standard models where
we might modify learning rates

258
00:12:31,490 --> 00:12:34,970
to be lower all the way back
at the bottom of a network,

259
00:12:34,970 --> 00:12:36,890
to be gentle with
those embedding layers

260
00:12:36,890 --> 00:12:40,310
and help them learn effectively.

261
00:12:40,310 --> 00:12:42,800
But this is really
trouble because if we

262
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:45,980
had a good embedding
layer at the start,

263
00:12:45,980 --> 00:12:49,210
those subsequent layers could
be much easier to learn.

264
00:12:49,210 --> 00:12:53,120

265
00:12:53,120 --> 00:13:00,350
So ultimately, in order to
approach this challenge,

266
00:13:00,350 --> 00:13:03,180
we came along with a
discernibility hypothesis.

267
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:05,810
In other words, this
boiled down to the theory

268
00:13:05,810 --> 00:13:09,140
that low dimensional
vectors, more than anything,

269
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:11,720
needed to be able
to discern features.

270
00:13:11,720 --> 00:13:16,490
And that doesn't sound like
a very strong assertion.

271
00:13:16,490 --> 00:13:21,530
And we started with a really,
really, really low bar

272
00:13:21,530 --> 00:13:26,330
and assume that the most common
features needed to be the most

273
00:13:26,330 --> 00:13:27,480
discernible features.

274
00:13:27,480 --> 00:13:29,210
So if we're stuck
with a lower dimension

275
00:13:29,210 --> 00:13:31,340
and we can't give
everything a one hot vector

276
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:34,460
to be told apart
very well, then we

277
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:37,040
might want to give the
more clear vectors which

278
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:42,410
have more dimensional
independencies to those features

279
00:13:42,410 --> 00:13:45,800
which appear most frequently
and could stand to confuse

280
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:48,740
models the most.

281
00:13:48,740 --> 00:13:51,650
This hypothesis led us directly
to develop the bit-cipher

282
00:13:51,650 --> 00:13:57,740
algorithm, which is really just
a scheme for assigning vectors

283
00:13:57,740 --> 00:13:59,160
of 0's and 1's.

284
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:02,610
Nothing too crazy in terms of
what we're attempting to do.

285
00:14:02,610 --> 00:14:06,290
In the figure at right here,
the order of vector assignment

286
00:14:06,290 --> 00:14:08,340
is by row from top to bottom.

287
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:13,940
And this is on a 5 dimension,
5 bit-vector system.

288
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:18,260
The first 5 from bottom
are those one-hot vectors.

289
00:14:18,260 --> 00:14:20,990
Past that point, you'll
see two-hot vectors,

290
00:14:20,990 --> 00:14:24,080
but they're a little
bit less darkly shaded,

291
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:27,240
indicating the way that we
actually utilize the system.

292
00:14:27,240 --> 00:14:30,880
In other words, we normalize
them to have unit sum.

293
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:33,710

294
00:14:33,710 --> 00:14:36,440
What I hope you
can see from this

295
00:14:36,440 --> 00:14:40,520
is that the bit-cipher algorithm
generalizes one-hot vectors

296
00:14:40,520 --> 00:14:42,750
to low dimensions.

297
00:14:42,750 --> 00:14:48,050
And as a result, we can work
from a very sparse feature set

298
00:14:48,050 --> 00:14:53,540
and explore dimensionalities
as a controlled phenomenon.

299
00:14:53,540 --> 00:14:56,940
And this assignment is
incredibly naive, too.

300
00:14:56,940 --> 00:14:59,930
That's the other thing that
I want you to see, as well.

301
00:14:59,930 --> 00:15:01,910
That this discernibility
hypothesis

302
00:15:01,910 --> 00:15:04,910
does not create any meaningful
correlations between tokens

303
00:15:04,910 --> 00:15:06,060
that behave similarly.

304
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:10,120
So if you've got the upper
and lower case of a word,

305
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:12,550
their vectors aren't going
to capture those similarities

306
00:15:12,550 --> 00:15:14,080
according to the bit-cipher.

307
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:16,150
It's really just going
to try and make sure

308
00:15:16,150 --> 00:15:18,100
that those features
are distinguishable

309
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:19,983
in a low dimensional
space and that the most

310
00:15:19,983 --> 00:15:21,400
distinguishable
features are those

311
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:24,700
which appear most commonly.

312
00:15:24,700 --> 00:15:28,425
This was enough to do a
surprising amount of work.

313
00:15:28,425 --> 00:15:31,600

314
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:37,780
So with some scheme
for a deterministic low

315
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:40,690
dimensionalization
procedure, we were then

316
00:15:40,690 --> 00:15:43,420
able to utilize this
solution that we had actually

317
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:45,160
developed previously.

318
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:48,310
So this was actually the real
motivator for a lot of the work

319
00:15:48,310 --> 00:15:51,370
that you're seeing today, while
it might seem like it's just

320
00:15:51,370 --> 00:15:52,495
a checkpoint in the middle.

321
00:15:52,495 --> 00:15:55,612

322
00:15:55,612 --> 00:15:57,980
Provided bit-cipher
produces decent embedding.

323
00:15:57,980 --> 00:16:01,343
We can ask, can other layers
be non-randomly initialized?

324
00:16:01,343 --> 00:16:03,010
In other words, without
gradient descent

325
00:16:03,010 --> 00:16:05,380
or back propagation or other
gradient-based iterative

326
00:16:05,380 --> 00:16:07,330
algorithms.

327
00:16:07,330 --> 00:16:11,950
This equation came about
from analysis of word2vec

328
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:15,640
with the original softmax
activation function.

329
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:21,130
And much like
other articulations

330
00:16:21,130 --> 00:16:23,980
of the word2vec,
family of embeddings

331
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:28,180
came up with differential
solutions that

332
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:30,830
depended on
co-occurrence matrices,

333
00:16:30,830 --> 00:16:32,630
we formalized this
as a question.

334
00:16:32,630 --> 00:16:36,460
Is there a way to take a
co-occurrence matrix, F,

335
00:16:36,460 --> 00:16:42,160
in this equation here, and
convert it with some weights,

336
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:45,790
some denominators by
rho into something

337
00:16:45,790 --> 00:16:50,880
that warms up a single
layer feed forward

338
00:16:50,880 --> 00:16:52,800
in a neural network.

339
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:59,160
And ultimately, this k minus 1
over k term here and this sum

340
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:01,020
is really just
expressing something

341
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:03,240
like conditional probability.

342
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:07,800
Like conditional probability,
because k minus 1 over k

343
00:17:07,800 --> 00:17:13,290
is a wrinkle that says that
as the number of features

344
00:17:13,290 --> 00:17:15,990
increases, in other
words, the context window

345
00:17:15,990 --> 00:17:18,990
increases in a
block transformer,

346
00:17:18,990 --> 00:17:21,599
then the warm
start that we could

347
00:17:21,599 --> 00:17:25,410
apply to start off a neural
network without a randomness,

348
00:17:25,410 --> 00:17:27,990
randomness entirely
determined by the vectors

349
00:17:27,990 --> 00:17:34,290
underneath nearing whatever
direction it's going.

350
00:17:34,290 --> 00:17:37,080
All we have to do is
compute some co-occurrences

351
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:38,650
between inputs and outputs.

352
00:17:38,650 --> 00:17:41,557
And I don't mean necessarily
standard co-occurrences

353
00:17:41,557 --> 00:17:43,890
that you might have learned
about a long time ago, which

354
00:17:43,890 --> 00:17:44,920
depend on a radius.

355
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:49,680
I mean, whatever your inputs
are, whatever your outputs are,

356
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:52,800
you take their sum
of outer products

357
00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:57,210
and you get a co-occurrence
matrix of inputs and outputs.

358
00:17:57,210 --> 00:18:02,070
And that can then be utilized
to initialize your layer

359
00:18:02,070 --> 00:18:06,750
in that neural network to be
vastly more performant than what

360
00:18:06,750 --> 00:18:09,930
you'd get by a random
initialization.

361
00:18:09,930 --> 00:18:13,470
This was a strong
motivator for us.

362
00:18:13,470 --> 00:18:16,320
This was just for a
single layer model,

363
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:21,450
but it depended on the softmax
function for activation.

364
00:18:21,450 --> 00:18:24,480
And the softmax function,
as an activation function,

365
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:29,790
we knew is also necessary
for self-attention features.

366
00:18:29,790 --> 00:18:33,060
And this meant that if we
could put self-attention

367
00:18:33,060 --> 00:18:36,310
into some kind of a standard
form with this equation,

368
00:18:36,310 --> 00:18:42,070
just like a single layer, then
we could apply the same solution

369
00:18:42,070 --> 00:18:43,570
with one catch.

370
00:18:43,570 --> 00:18:46,390
That catch is
specifically that we

371
00:18:46,390 --> 00:18:48,790
don't know what the targets
are for self-attention.

372
00:18:48,790 --> 00:18:51,460
There is no target vector y.

373
00:18:51,460 --> 00:18:54,370
The thing that you're
trying to predict,

374
00:18:54,370 --> 00:18:55,930
which position is
the one that you

375
00:18:55,930 --> 00:18:58,390
want to weight most strongly.

376
00:18:58,390 --> 00:19:01,450
And so in order to
apply this solution

377
00:19:01,450 --> 00:19:04,840
for a self-attention model, we
had to do some more analysis.

378
00:19:04,840 --> 00:19:08,320
And that's in the reference
number 1, which is all the way

379
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:10,840
back up in the first slide
if you want to see it.

380
00:19:10,840 --> 00:19:14,590
But that derives a
differential criterion,

381
00:19:14,590 --> 00:19:17,740
an analog for the
single layer solution

382
00:19:17,740 --> 00:19:21,790
that tells us what the targets
of that kind of self-attention

383
00:19:21,790 --> 00:19:22,550
actually are.

384
00:19:22,550 --> 00:19:26,200
The hidden targets, the weights
that you're trying to create,

385
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:28,660
which really are just
about making sure

386
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:31,240
that the layer
above self-attention

387
00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:36,260
has some unsurprising
things coming towards it.

388
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:38,230
The self-attention layer
is really just trying

389
00:19:38,230 --> 00:19:40,030
to massage the vectors,
so that way they

390
00:19:40,030 --> 00:19:44,560
look like something that the
next layer above expects.

391
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:48,440
Aside from that, though, it's a
much more in-depth conversation.

392
00:19:48,440 --> 00:19:57,030
The point, though, is that for
the model in this picture here,

393
00:19:57,030 --> 00:20:04,320
we can now start off with
vectors x that are not random.

394
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:10,440
We can use those vectors x
to initialize non-randomly

395
00:20:10,440 --> 00:20:13,440
the parameters in w, the
self-attention matrix,

396
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:15,990
and then use that
going up the network

397
00:20:15,990 --> 00:20:19,500
to initialize the
parameters in u since it's

398
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:22,170
just a feed forward layer
with whatever self-attention

399
00:20:22,170 --> 00:20:24,240
is giving it his weights.

400
00:20:24,240 --> 00:20:27,750
And then whatever that
produces, the hidden state h,

401
00:20:27,750 --> 00:20:33,780
we can use that with the actual
targets after the output layer

402
00:20:33,780 --> 00:20:38,850
to warm up the matrix O.
And you might say OK, well,

403
00:20:38,850 --> 00:20:43,200
how did you figure out what
those hidden targets are?

404
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:48,540
Like you had to have an output
for the U matrix to try and hit.

405
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:54,300
That too is something
that the bit-cipher

406
00:20:54,300 --> 00:20:57,310
can provide in the form
of label embeddings.

407
00:20:57,310 --> 00:20:59,280
In other words, low
dimensional targets

408
00:20:59,280 --> 00:21:02,250
of the thing that is downstream
that you're trying to hit

409
00:21:02,250 --> 00:21:04,260
the language model's output.

410
00:21:04,260 --> 00:21:07,650
So similarly, we can
warm start the U matrix

411
00:21:07,650 --> 00:21:11,290
in terms of those
bit-cipher label embeddings.

412
00:21:11,290 --> 00:21:15,880

413
00:21:15,880 --> 00:21:19,300
So in this view, the aim is
to show how simple and general

414
00:21:19,300 --> 00:21:22,450
the single layer softmax
activated solution is to apply.

415
00:21:22,450 --> 00:21:25,780
It's really just no more
challenging than computing

416
00:21:25,780 --> 00:21:29,920
conditional probability
given inputs and outputs.

417
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:31,757
It's fast.

418
00:21:31,757 --> 00:21:33,340
It's something that
you can distribute

419
00:21:33,340 --> 00:21:35,350
in terms of processing.

420
00:21:35,350 --> 00:21:39,590
And it's very, very general.

421
00:21:39,590 --> 00:21:44,390
So this is essentially
the process

422
00:21:44,390 --> 00:21:50,300
that we're using in order to
warm up the W and U matrix.

423
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:53,680
There's the U matrix there.

424
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:54,830
Starts out as zeros.

425
00:21:54,830 --> 00:21:56,540
So in other words, nothing.

426
00:21:56,540 --> 00:22:00,070
No random values,
no weights anywhere

427
00:22:00,070 --> 00:22:03,910
over the data, which
is just borrowing

428
00:22:03,910 --> 00:22:06,640
the dimension of this
gigantic Y matrix that

429
00:22:06,640 --> 00:22:11,950
has all of the targets in
it for the entire data set.

430
00:22:11,950 --> 00:22:14,110
We simply just take
the outer products

431
00:22:14,110 --> 00:22:17,350
of whatever the hidden state
the input to that layer is,

432
00:22:17,350 --> 00:22:19,900
assuming that the
lower layers beneath it

433
00:22:19,900 --> 00:22:25,120
are also warmed up with whatever
the targets for that layer are.

434
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:28,630
Following that, it's really
just about normalization

435
00:22:28,630 --> 00:22:31,000
and a logarithmic
transformation.

436
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,190
And that logarithm
really just emerges

437
00:22:33,190 --> 00:22:37,360
as a result of being an inverse
to the exponential function,

438
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:43,060
which is a part of softmax,
pretty much all of softmax.

439
00:22:43,060 --> 00:22:47,800
And that's really
what brought us here.

440
00:22:47,800 --> 00:22:50,410
So what does warm
starting a network do?

441
00:22:50,410 --> 00:22:56,500
This is going back to before
we had the bit-cipher algorithm

442
00:22:56,500 --> 00:22:58,570
for dimensionality reduction.

443
00:22:58,570 --> 00:23:00,730
And we started out
by just saying, OK,

444
00:23:00,730 --> 00:23:06,280
if we take a simple, simple
language model that only looks

445
00:23:06,280 --> 00:23:11,200
at a radius of traditional
co-occurrences as features,

446
00:23:11,200 --> 00:23:14,470
we can concatenate those
vectors and feed them forward

447
00:23:14,470 --> 00:23:17,380
for a language model's output.

448
00:23:17,380 --> 00:23:23,530
A completely random start, a
cold start to a language model

449
00:23:23,530 --> 00:23:27,340
is really just the size of
the vocabulary in perplexity.

450
00:23:27,340 --> 00:23:32,320
And those three lines here
for a few different radii

451
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:34,780
are demonstrating that point
with the point all the way

452
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:41,020
at the top left-hand corner
of this figure, cold starts.

453
00:23:41,020 --> 00:23:43,790
In any of those cases when
the warm start is applied,

454
00:23:43,790 --> 00:23:48,590
the perplexity is immediately,
automatically lower.

455
00:23:48,590 --> 00:23:53,240
And furthermore,
the trajectories

456
00:23:53,240 --> 00:23:58,070
that the updates follow continue
in the same learning rate

457
00:23:58,070 --> 00:24:01,100
and the same time
to perform better

458
00:24:01,100 --> 00:24:05,690
than models that
were started cold.

459
00:24:05,690 --> 00:24:07,820
If you have an early
stopping criterion,

460
00:24:07,820 --> 00:24:13,310
similarly, early stopping
will more than just generally

461
00:24:13,310 --> 00:24:15,560
engage first.

462
00:24:15,560 --> 00:24:19,010
And with a higher perplexity.

463
00:24:19,010 --> 00:24:22,100
So this was the first
indication that we

464
00:24:22,100 --> 00:24:24,430
had figured out something
that's very useful.

465
00:24:24,430 --> 00:24:28,640

466
00:24:28,640 --> 00:24:31,950
I saw some folks on Slido
say they're confused.

467
00:24:31,950 --> 00:24:34,450
They're asking, are we talking
about an alternative approach

468
00:24:34,450 --> 00:24:35,800
to self-attention?

469
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:36,520
We are.

470
00:24:36,520 --> 00:24:38,640
And that's all the
way back at slide 1.

471
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:41,180

472
00:24:41,180 --> 00:24:45,560
And it is the premise of
this whole conversation.

473
00:24:45,560 --> 00:24:50,180
So here in this modified
version of self-attention,

474
00:24:50,180 --> 00:24:53,840
you might normally expect to
do a comparison of your inputs,

475
00:24:53,840 --> 00:24:57,050
the matrix X. Whatever
your inputs are,

476
00:24:57,050 --> 00:24:58,790
they might be a whole
block of vectors

477
00:24:58,790 --> 00:25:00,540
or they might be--
this is self-attention.

478
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:02,915
It's not cross-attention where
you have different vectors

479
00:25:02,915 --> 00:25:05,960
that you're trying to attend.

480
00:25:05,960 --> 00:25:10,670
And forgetting about
the values, which

481
00:25:10,670 --> 00:25:15,830
for us is the U matrix,
the keys and queries which

482
00:25:15,830 --> 00:25:17,420
are the parameters
for self-attention

483
00:25:17,420 --> 00:25:18,170
are in the middle.

484
00:25:18,170 --> 00:25:25,270
They're in between the two
copies of the inputs X.

485
00:25:25,270 --> 00:25:28,690
Each of those, you can view
as some kind of a projection

486
00:25:28,690 --> 00:25:30,562
down to a dimension
where they can interact.

487
00:25:30,562 --> 00:25:32,020
And this is necessary
for something

488
00:25:32,020 --> 00:25:34,360
like cross-attention,
where you might

489
00:25:34,360 --> 00:25:37,000
have different
dimensionalities like X1 and X2

490
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:38,410
in two separate
groups of vectors

491
00:25:38,410 --> 00:25:41,350
if you're doing something
like machine translation.

492
00:25:41,350 --> 00:25:45,340
That's not necessary to
think about when you're just

493
00:25:45,340 --> 00:25:48,760
looking to do a standard
language model that

494
00:25:48,760 --> 00:25:52,660
has to predict the next output
according to the inputs, which

495
00:25:52,660 --> 00:25:54,846
are also outputs from
previous iterations.

496
00:25:54,846 --> 00:25:58,690

497
00:25:58,690 --> 00:25:59,960
Two insights here.

498
00:25:59,960 --> 00:26:04,030
One that multiplying
the key and query

499
00:26:04,030 --> 00:26:10,660
matrices Wk and Wq is just
another parameter matrix that's

500
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:12,610
implied.

501
00:26:12,610 --> 00:26:14,830
There aren't two
parameter matrices

502
00:26:14,830 --> 00:26:16,480
there in the middle
for self-attention

503
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:18,670
in any effective way.

504
00:26:18,670 --> 00:26:21,700
There is a common
dimension of comparison,

505
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:24,620
and that kind of just
moves stuff around.

506
00:26:24,620 --> 00:26:28,060
It creates degrees of
freedom so that optimization

507
00:26:28,060 --> 00:26:31,850
can figure out what's the best
weighting from comparisons.

508
00:26:31,850 --> 00:26:34,030
But the softmax
function is strictly

509
00:26:34,030 --> 00:26:40,060
operating on similarities
of that comparison space.

510
00:26:40,060 --> 00:26:43,380
It's not doing anything
with those similarities.

511
00:26:43,380 --> 00:26:44,840
It's just softmaxing them.

512
00:26:44,840 --> 00:26:46,060
It's just activating them.

513
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:50,650
So if it was a big similarity,
it's a big attention value.

514
00:26:50,650 --> 00:26:54,190
In this equation,
there's no transformation

515
00:26:54,190 --> 00:26:57,320
happening before those vectors
are multiplied together.

516
00:26:57,320 --> 00:26:58,740
Inner products.

517
00:26:58,740 --> 00:27:01,220
So those vectors
better be good vectors

518
00:27:01,220 --> 00:27:05,900
that you're starting with, X
and X transpose, the same thing.

519
00:27:05,900 --> 00:27:08,570
They better be vectors
that are comparable.

520
00:27:08,570 --> 00:27:10,490
They can't be vectors
from cross-attention

521
00:27:10,490 --> 00:27:12,720
where you're trying to translate
from one language to another

522
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:14,137
and they just don't
inner product.

523
00:27:14,137 --> 00:27:15,500
They're different dimensions.

524
00:27:15,500 --> 00:27:17,900
You could force it through
if they were two differently

525
00:27:17,900 --> 00:27:19,370
trained embedding layers.

526
00:27:19,370 --> 00:27:22,850
And they had the same
dimension with this mechanism.

527
00:27:22,850 --> 00:27:26,480
And if you didn't, you could
put those key and query matrices

528
00:27:26,480 --> 00:27:30,205
back in between the 2x
vectors, x blocks of vectors.

529
00:27:30,205 --> 00:27:33,180

530
00:27:33,180 --> 00:27:35,790
But a lot of what's going
on here in this talk

531
00:27:35,790 --> 00:27:43,550
is trying to simplify and make
more efficient the architectures

532
00:27:43,550 --> 00:27:46,370
that we need and the
mechanisms that they

533
00:27:46,370 --> 00:27:52,063
utilize given what we know
about how language functions.

534
00:27:52,063 --> 00:27:53,480
And that's a
critical piece there.

535
00:27:53,480 --> 00:27:55,260
We have assumptions
that we can make.

536
00:27:55,260 --> 00:27:57,170
If all we're doing
is autoregression,

537
00:27:57,170 --> 00:28:02,920
we don't need cross-attention
dimensionalization in between.

538
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:04,100
That'll be the theme.

539
00:28:04,100 --> 00:28:07,990
In other words, that
can we use knowledge

540
00:28:07,990 --> 00:28:10,210
that we have about
the way language

541
00:28:10,210 --> 00:28:14,080
functions to design better
versions of architectures

542
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:17,685
that meet the needs of language
instead of being simply general?

543
00:28:17,685 --> 00:28:20,400

544
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:22,640
Just good.

545
00:28:22,640 --> 00:28:23,490
This is important.

546
00:28:23,490 --> 00:28:25,698
So if there are any questions
here, it's a good time.

547
00:28:25,698 --> 00:28:31,120

548
00:28:31,120 --> 00:28:36,640
We are there and there.

549
00:28:36,640 --> 00:28:38,740
So we just talked briefly.

550
00:28:38,740 --> 00:28:39,902
This was for language.

551
00:28:39,902 --> 00:28:41,360
I'm thinking about
language models.

552
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:43,020
It's a really simple
language model.

553
00:28:43,020 --> 00:28:46,180
There's no
self-attention here yet.

554
00:28:46,180 --> 00:28:49,630
This is really just
evaluating that a warm start

555
00:28:49,630 --> 00:28:51,790
in either the blue,
green, or purple case

556
00:28:51,790 --> 00:28:55,210
does better than
its partner, which

557
00:28:55,210 --> 00:28:56,980
is a cold start of
the same architecture,

558
00:28:56,980 --> 00:28:59,790
same hyperparameters--

559
00:28:59,790 --> 00:29:04,440
orange, reddish, and brown.

560
00:29:04,440 --> 00:29:07,290
So three different models,
regardless of how long

561
00:29:07,290 --> 00:29:09,810
your context is
in each case here,

562
00:29:09,810 --> 00:29:13,470
we see that a model which has
a non-random initialization

563
00:29:13,470 --> 00:29:18,060
by the equation presented
two slides back from here

564
00:29:18,060 --> 00:29:23,560
starts a network of with
a much lower perplexity.

565
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:26,720

566
00:29:26,720 --> 00:29:30,470
The requirements to apply this
solution to a feedforward layer

567
00:29:30,470 --> 00:29:38,480
of parameters is simply
that your inputs should not

568
00:29:38,480 --> 00:29:40,910
have negative values.

569
00:29:40,910 --> 00:29:44,060
That's really all we
have to worry about.

570
00:29:44,060 --> 00:29:48,740
So it becomes really easy to
ask questions like, well, what

571
00:29:48,740 --> 00:29:50,990
happens when you apply
this to other data

572
00:29:50,990 --> 00:29:53,030
with non-negative values?

573
00:29:53,030 --> 00:29:55,820
Well, there's one
little catch that we

574
00:29:55,820 --> 00:29:57,380
had to think about
here in this case.

575
00:29:57,380 --> 00:30:01,310
And that is with the
bit-cipher or one-hot vectors,

576
00:30:01,310 --> 00:30:04,010
we're controlling the
norms of the inputs

577
00:30:04,010 --> 00:30:08,540
with standard embeddings,
with MNIST, for example,

578
00:30:08,540 --> 00:30:12,020
when you're trying to
predict a handwritten digits

579
00:30:12,020 --> 00:30:14,990
0 through 9 value.

580
00:30:14,990 --> 00:30:19,160
You don't get to assume
necessarily that all inputs have

581
00:30:19,160 --> 00:30:21,410
the same norm.

582
00:30:21,410 --> 00:30:24,770
You can normalize the inputs,
but it doesn't necessarily

583
00:30:24,770 --> 00:30:27,290
make sense to normalize
them to one when you're

584
00:30:27,290 --> 00:30:29,180
looking at images, for example.

585
00:30:29,180 --> 00:30:30,500
They're non-negative.

586
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:34,920
They have 0 through 255,
for example, in MNIST.

587
00:30:34,920 --> 00:30:38,190
And as a result, we
can put these data

588
00:30:38,190 --> 00:30:41,910
through that same warm start.

589
00:30:41,910 --> 00:30:45,330
Now, one little caveat
here I've alluded

590
00:30:45,330 --> 00:30:51,090
to about the norms of
vectors is that we don't

591
00:30:51,090 --> 00:30:53,100
know what that value of k is.

592
00:30:53,100 --> 00:30:55,175
In other words-- let me go back.

593
00:30:55,175 --> 00:30:58,230

594
00:30:58,230 --> 00:31:02,120
You could look at
it here or here.

595
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:05,810
That's the number of
features per prediction,

596
00:31:05,810 --> 00:31:09,560
which if you're looking at
unit normed word vectors,

597
00:31:09,560 --> 00:31:14,570
is however big your context
window is, because they all

598
00:31:14,570 --> 00:31:17,420
have unit norm and
there's k of them.

599
00:31:17,420 --> 00:31:20,210
But if you're looking
at just an image,

600
00:31:20,210 --> 00:31:23,660
it's not clear if it's a
composition of multiple vectors,

601
00:31:23,660 --> 00:31:28,130
if it's one vector, and how many
it is, if it is a composition.

602
00:31:28,130 --> 00:31:29,440
It just has a norm.

603
00:31:29,440 --> 00:31:32,650

604
00:31:32,650 --> 00:31:35,860
In application to
data like that,

605
00:31:35,860 --> 00:31:41,410
that is what k becomes, the
average norm of an input.

606
00:31:41,410 --> 00:31:43,600
And I'm regretting not
putting a graph in this,

607
00:31:43,600 --> 00:31:47,080
but the paper that discusses
this shows that in the MNIST

608
00:31:47,080 --> 00:31:51,130
data set, the exact
optimal value of k

609
00:31:51,130 --> 00:31:54,250
is the average
norm of the inputs,

610
00:31:54,250 --> 00:31:57,960
however you've
preprocessed them.

611
00:31:57,960 --> 00:32:00,270
And that's how we generally
apply this rule when

612
00:32:00,270 --> 00:32:04,590
we're warm starting systems and
we don't have unit norm vectors.

613
00:32:04,590 --> 00:32:08,100
And it was learned from studying
this model's application--

614
00:32:08,100 --> 00:32:12,800
this solution's application
to non-linguistic data.

615
00:32:12,800 --> 00:32:17,510
But as mentioned, the purpose
was always towards language.

616
00:32:17,510 --> 00:32:21,210

617
00:32:21,210 --> 00:32:24,660
So longer context
windows in principle

618
00:32:24,660 --> 00:32:27,870
should provide models with more
information than shorter context

619
00:32:27,870 --> 00:32:30,000
windows.

620
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:32,250
This means one should
expect that models

621
00:32:32,250 --> 00:32:37,590
perform better when context
window length is longer,

622
00:32:37,590 --> 00:32:40,050
theoretically.

623
00:32:40,050 --> 00:32:42,060
And this is
essentially the reason

624
00:32:42,060 --> 00:32:44,620
for why self-attention
was initially developed.

625
00:32:44,620 --> 00:32:47,430
Researchers wanted to improve
language models and context

626
00:32:47,430 --> 00:32:50,640
windows, providing more
information were seen as the key

627
00:32:50,640 --> 00:32:51,600
to that.

628
00:32:51,600 --> 00:32:55,080
In other words, the more
features, the more information,

629
00:32:55,080 --> 00:33:01,780
the more flexibility a model
can have and expressivity.

630
00:33:01,780 --> 00:33:03,430
However, without
feature weights,

631
00:33:03,430 --> 00:33:06,070
models didn't simply get better
with long context windows,

632
00:33:06,070 --> 00:33:09,010
and feature weights
and self-attention

633
00:33:09,010 --> 00:33:11,060
were hypothesized to be needed.

634
00:33:11,060 --> 00:33:14,800
And this was proven back in
2017 with the transformer

635
00:33:14,800 --> 00:33:15,860
architecture.

636
00:33:15,860 --> 00:33:18,530

637
00:33:18,530 --> 00:33:21,440
In moving towards
self-attention and transformer,

638
00:33:21,440 --> 00:33:25,370
though, the primacy of the
transformer architecture's block

639
00:33:25,370 --> 00:33:30,800
context model casts a shadow
over the use of other context

640
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:31,910
models.

641
00:33:31,910 --> 00:33:35,120
So, for example, if
I were to ask here,

642
00:33:35,120 --> 00:33:41,360
is it clear to everyone that the
standard self-attention block

643
00:33:41,360 --> 00:33:45,620
model of context is different
than the traditional notion

644
00:33:45,620 --> 00:33:47,630
of co-occurrences,
which use a radius that

645
00:33:47,630 --> 00:33:51,410
is not positionally anchored?

646
00:33:51,410 --> 00:33:54,410
It is the context model,
the positional anchoring

647
00:33:54,410 --> 00:33:59,180
of the block context model
that gives it its information.

648
00:33:59,180 --> 00:34:06,140
It is not in all
likelihood anything else.

649
00:34:06,140 --> 00:34:10,350
Now what you do with that
context model matters.

650
00:34:10,350 --> 00:34:12,440
You can't just take
those vectors in a block,

651
00:34:12,440 --> 00:34:15,139
add them together, and expect
a feed forward to do well.

652
00:34:15,139 --> 00:34:17,120
That's where self-attention
is needed in order

653
00:34:17,120 --> 00:34:23,219
to figure out which vector needs
the best weight, most weight.

654
00:34:23,219 --> 00:34:26,190
So what you'll also see
in the architectures that

655
00:34:26,190 --> 00:34:29,130
are based on what
I've already presented

656
00:34:29,130 --> 00:34:31,469
is that we're
interested to explore

657
00:34:31,469 --> 00:34:34,500
how different models of
context for language models

658
00:34:34,500 --> 00:34:39,510
can be integrated in general,
because they each provide

659
00:34:39,510 --> 00:34:41,110
different information.

660
00:34:41,110 --> 00:34:44,040
And we all know that the
standard transformers block

661
00:34:44,040 --> 00:34:47,489
model of context requires a
ridiculous amount of information

662
00:34:47,489 --> 00:34:52,739
and data in order to
become effectively trained.

663
00:34:52,739 --> 00:34:59,870
So the current spate of
contexts that we use,

664
00:34:59,870 --> 00:35:02,540
top there might be the standard
transformer context that

665
00:35:02,540 --> 00:35:04,460
has a fixed positional block.

666
00:35:04,460 --> 00:35:06,230
And it takes the
first 10 tokens,

667
00:35:06,230 --> 00:35:10,430
for example, the second 10
tokens, and the third 10 tokens

668
00:35:10,430 --> 00:35:12,800
each in different blocks.

669
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:17,870
And each of those is a group
of contextualizing vectors.

670
00:35:17,870 --> 00:35:22,280
The second one there that you
see with the r as a subscript

671
00:35:22,280 --> 00:35:24,930
is a radial model because
those do different things.

672
00:35:24,930 --> 00:35:26,600
In other words,
rather than assume

673
00:35:26,600 --> 00:35:30,440
you're looking at the first
10 or the nth 10 features,

674
00:35:30,440 --> 00:35:31,550
you pick a radius.

675
00:35:31,550 --> 00:35:36,680
And you say, what are the last
r features, the last r vectors?

676
00:35:36,680 --> 00:35:39,020
That can also have an
attention distribution,

677
00:35:39,020 --> 00:35:41,060
a self-attention
distribution according

678
00:35:41,060 --> 00:35:44,810
to the exact same model
that's being presented.

679
00:35:44,810 --> 00:35:48,260
It produces an entirely
separate context

680
00:35:48,260 --> 00:35:51,530
in the state, whatever
you want to call it,

681
00:35:51,530 --> 00:35:55,340
which can be conjoined
with the block model

682
00:35:55,340 --> 00:36:00,870
to articulate features and be
given to an output layer that

683
00:36:00,870 --> 00:36:06,850
knows what to do with them
when each has different values.

684
00:36:06,850 --> 00:36:09,160
The concatenation of those
different context models

685
00:36:09,160 --> 00:36:11,000
keeps the information separate.

686
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:14,890
So the output layer can decide
which portion of the context

687
00:36:14,890 --> 00:36:18,700
is useful for the prediction.

688
00:36:18,700 --> 00:36:23,080
This last one is getting really
traditional at the bottom.

689
00:36:23,080 --> 00:36:25,840
It's what I refer to
as a document model.

690
00:36:25,840 --> 00:36:31,780
If you've ever implemented
something like a Naive Bayes

691
00:36:31,780 --> 00:36:35,440
classifier or a term frequency
inverse document frequency

692
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:38,690
model, that's essentially
what a document model is.

693
00:36:38,690 --> 00:36:42,640
Add up your vectors,
you get something.

694
00:36:42,640 --> 00:36:45,680
Is it going to be the best
for predicting the next token?

695
00:36:45,680 --> 00:36:46,910
Absolutely not.

696
00:36:46,910 --> 00:36:49,360
However, it's always different.

697
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:51,460
And what that means
is that even if you

698
00:36:51,460 --> 00:36:55,480
wrap to the next block between
the radial and the document

699
00:36:55,480 --> 00:36:59,140
models, you have a unique
context vector, even if you're

700
00:36:59,140 --> 00:37:02,860
looking at the exact same block
because the document has grown

701
00:37:02,860 --> 00:37:05,090
and the radius just says,
what are the last three?

702
00:37:05,090 --> 00:37:07,360
What are the last 10?

703
00:37:07,360 --> 00:37:10,935
As a result, when you
incorporate different models

704
00:37:10,935 --> 00:37:12,310
of context, you
don't really have

705
00:37:12,310 --> 00:37:14,300
to say that there's a
finite context window.

706
00:37:14,300 --> 00:37:17,110
It might not be very
good to make predictions

707
00:37:17,110 --> 00:37:19,790
past the first block, but
that might be about how much

708
00:37:19,790 --> 00:37:22,610
data you've used,
and it might be

709
00:37:22,610 --> 00:37:24,920
about the hyperparameters
for each one of those models

710
00:37:24,920 --> 00:37:26,225
that you're applying.

711
00:37:26,225 --> 00:37:28,600
In other words, the radius
and the block size like usual.

712
00:37:28,600 --> 00:37:32,810

713
00:37:32,810 --> 00:37:35,420
So far, the only embeddings
that I've suggested

714
00:37:35,420 --> 00:37:37,320
are from this
bit-cipher algorithm.

715
00:37:37,320 --> 00:37:41,090
And as I've
expressed there, they

716
00:37:41,090 --> 00:37:42,890
don't capture any
useful similarities

717
00:37:42,890 --> 00:37:44,780
between similar tokens.

718
00:37:44,780 --> 00:37:47,330
The bit-cipher
algorithm, it doesn't

719
00:37:47,330 --> 00:37:50,110
care if you're looking at the
uppercase or lowercase version

720
00:37:50,110 --> 00:37:50,610
of a word.

721
00:37:50,610 --> 00:37:52,527
It doesn't see them as
bearing any similarity,

722
00:37:52,527 --> 00:37:56,870
even though there might
be used very similarly.

723
00:37:56,870 --> 00:38:01,280
So how can you
utilize the bit-cipher

724
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:05,900
to create vectors
for tokens that

725
00:38:05,900 --> 00:38:08,510
have meaningful
similarities between words

726
00:38:08,510 --> 00:38:11,420
that are used similarly?

727
00:38:11,420 --> 00:38:16,500
And this is just backing off
to the traditional methods

728
00:38:16,500 --> 00:38:21,170
once again taking co-occurrences
of bit-cipher vectors

729
00:38:21,170 --> 00:38:24,860
with whatever is there
at the middle or center

730
00:38:24,860 --> 00:38:25,930
of a co-occurrence model.

731
00:38:25,930 --> 00:38:29,410

732
00:38:29,410 --> 00:38:32,140
Normally, if you think
about one hot vectors,

733
00:38:32,140 --> 00:38:36,100
a co-occurrence matrix is
really just the same thing,

734
00:38:36,100 --> 00:38:39,190
except now we just
have smaller vectors

735
00:38:39,190 --> 00:38:44,080
with different dimensions
on, so to speak.

736
00:38:44,080 --> 00:38:47,440
And we normalize
after concatenating

737
00:38:47,440 --> 00:38:52,330
these blocks of different
radii from the bit-cipher

738
00:38:52,330 --> 00:38:57,700
to match the original input
requirements that we discovered

739
00:38:57,700 --> 00:39:00,280
for the warm start solution.

740
00:39:00,280 --> 00:39:03,310
And that enables us to use
these just like we would

741
00:39:03,310 --> 00:39:08,050
the original bit-cipher
vectors, except now,

742
00:39:08,050 --> 00:39:11,260
just from the usual
co-occurrence statistics,

743
00:39:11,260 --> 00:39:15,430
you'll see that capital
word and lowercase word

744
00:39:15,430 --> 00:39:17,160
have a lot of common usage.

745
00:39:17,160 --> 00:39:21,280
And you know this works because
you've seen co-occurrences

746
00:39:21,280 --> 00:39:22,520
for a very long time.

747
00:39:22,520 --> 00:39:25,450
And while they
might not normally

748
00:39:25,450 --> 00:39:29,080
be useful in our applications
these days with deep learning,

749
00:39:29,080 --> 00:39:33,100
they can be imparted through
the bit-cipher algorithm

750
00:39:33,100 --> 00:39:35,160
to prescribe vectors as well.

751
00:39:35,160 --> 00:39:41,620

752
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:44,950
So here's where things
start paying out in terms

753
00:39:44,950 --> 00:39:47,120
of speed and efficiency.

754
00:39:47,120 --> 00:39:51,170

755
00:39:51,170 --> 00:39:54,500
If you only have one
layer of self-attention,

756
00:39:54,500 --> 00:39:57,410
then that means
that you don't need

757
00:39:57,410 --> 00:40:01,490
to worry about whatever weird
expressive stuff is happening

758
00:40:01,490 --> 00:40:04,040
that similar inputs
might have slightly

759
00:40:04,040 --> 00:40:07,110
different hidden states.

760
00:40:07,110 --> 00:40:13,050
Since that first layer is just
a set of static word embeddings,

761
00:40:13,050 --> 00:40:15,660
the self-attention
layer is working off

762
00:40:15,660 --> 00:40:18,150
of static word embeddings.

763
00:40:18,150 --> 00:40:20,700
And that means
each pair of words

764
00:40:20,700 --> 00:40:25,860
have a fixed comparison
given static word embeddings.

765
00:40:25,860 --> 00:40:30,570
And that means if you want to
compute the quadratic features

766
00:40:30,570 --> 00:40:34,080
of self-attention, you can just
pre-compute them and pull them

767
00:40:34,080 --> 00:40:36,240
from memory.

768
00:40:36,240 --> 00:40:38,460
This caching of
vector comparisons

769
00:40:38,460 --> 00:40:42,960
is essentially reducing the
self-attention layer's cost

770
00:40:42,960 --> 00:40:46,230
from quadratic to
linear, since those

771
00:40:46,230 --> 00:40:48,090
values that we're
using to weight

772
00:40:48,090 --> 00:40:51,420
the vectors for the
feedforward layer

773
00:40:51,420 --> 00:40:55,570
no longer require
comparison across the block.

774
00:40:55,570 --> 00:40:57,840
They're already compared.

775
00:40:57,840 --> 00:41:01,860
So when our vectors are static,
which is at inference time,

776
00:41:01,860 --> 00:41:07,180
and if we're not learning
the embedding layers

777
00:41:07,180 --> 00:41:12,100
parameters with iterative
differential updates,

778
00:41:12,100 --> 00:41:16,090
then not only do we have
to not track gradients

779
00:41:16,090 --> 00:41:18,580
for the embedding
layer, but we don't even

780
00:41:18,580 --> 00:41:20,300
have to compute the
vector comparisons.

781
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:22,480
We can pre-compute them
and just load them,

782
00:41:22,480 --> 00:41:24,900
which is much, much faster.

783
00:41:24,900 --> 00:41:31,560

784
00:41:31,560 --> 00:41:34,830
So we can reduce a lot of--

785
00:41:34,830 --> 00:41:37,033
all the inference
and training costs.

786
00:41:37,033 --> 00:41:39,450
Not all of the training costs,
some of the training costs,

787
00:41:39,450 --> 00:41:41,242
because if we want to
update those vectors,

788
00:41:41,242 --> 00:41:45,240
then we can't assume
cached comparisons.

789
00:41:45,240 --> 00:41:48,390
But it's a huge cost savings.

790
00:41:48,390 --> 00:41:51,540
This means that we can train
these self-attentive feedforward

791
00:41:51,540 --> 00:41:57,060
unit models very quickly and
with good initializations.

792
00:41:57,060 --> 00:41:59,910
But there are some other
things that we immediately

793
00:41:59,910 --> 00:42:01,590
observed while
developing these models,

794
00:42:01,590 --> 00:42:07,620
and that is the lack of
randomization produced models

795
00:42:07,620 --> 00:42:10,360
which were quite effective
even on small data.

796
00:42:10,360 --> 00:42:12,570
Now it doesn't mean that
training on small data

797
00:42:12,570 --> 00:42:14,622
will let you generalize
to everything else that's

798
00:42:14,622 --> 00:42:15,580
out there in the world.

799
00:42:15,580 --> 00:42:17,413
In other words, training
on a small data set

800
00:42:17,413 --> 00:42:20,940
might produce a model which has
a surprisingly low perplexity

801
00:42:20,940 --> 00:42:22,530
on its training set.

802
00:42:22,530 --> 00:42:24,175
But it doesn't mean
that you're going

803
00:42:24,175 --> 00:42:26,550
to be able to generalize and
have a language model that's

804
00:42:26,550 --> 00:42:29,580
talking well from just hearing
a couple of thousand tokens.

805
00:42:29,580 --> 00:42:32,470
It does mean it will know
that couple of thousand tokens

806
00:42:32,470 --> 00:42:33,900
very well, very quickly.

807
00:42:33,900 --> 00:42:38,310

808
00:42:38,310 --> 00:42:43,620
But there is a challenge with
using self-attention still,

809
00:42:43,620 --> 00:42:47,880
and that is the fact that
the block model of context

810
00:42:47,880 --> 00:42:53,610
often is not fully utilized
since many documents are

811
00:42:53,610 --> 00:42:58,950
shorter than long context
models or long context windows.

812
00:42:58,950 --> 00:43:02,690
And these days, there are
exceptionally long context

813
00:43:02,690 --> 00:43:03,190
windows.

814
00:43:03,190 --> 00:43:05,250
I'm not even
talking about those.

815
00:43:05,250 --> 00:43:07,875
Many of the language modeling
benchmarks simply don't even go

816
00:43:07,875 --> 00:43:10,500
out to 1,000 words when it comes
to contexts and you're looking

817
00:43:10,500 --> 00:43:14,640
at a document to predict.

818
00:43:14,640 --> 00:43:19,810
So this has been a
problem for a while.

819
00:43:19,810 --> 00:43:22,140
And it means that
if you're going

820
00:43:22,140 --> 00:43:23,730
to pad your short
documents, you're

821
00:43:23,730 --> 00:43:28,920
going to waste a lot of
prediction on those paddings.

822
00:43:28,920 --> 00:43:32,790
A lot of computation gets lost
just for null information,

823
00:43:32,790 --> 00:43:34,680
essentially.

824
00:43:34,680 --> 00:43:39,810
And the way that this is
often relieved in some groups

825
00:43:39,810 --> 00:43:44,460
and to great effect is
by packing long contexts.

826
00:43:44,460 --> 00:43:48,390
So, for example, if you've got
a 100,000 token context window,

827
00:43:48,390 --> 00:43:51,410
most documents will not
be 100,000 tokens long.

828
00:43:51,410 --> 00:43:54,270
What do you do with the rest of
that long context if you want

829
00:43:54,270 --> 00:43:57,780
to use 1,000 tokens
of good training data?

830
00:43:57,780 --> 00:44:01,273
You fill out the other 99,000
tokens with a bunch of other

831
00:44:01,273 --> 00:44:03,690
random documents that don't
belong anywhere near the first

832
00:44:03,690 --> 00:44:04,190
one.

833
00:44:04,190 --> 00:44:07,050
That's called packing.

834
00:44:07,050 --> 00:44:14,040
Packing can be utilized without
impacting different documents

835
00:44:14,040 --> 00:44:16,530
with each other, without
contaminating the information

836
00:44:16,530 --> 00:44:17,750
between documents.

837
00:44:17,750 --> 00:44:21,970
And that takes a lot of
work, but it can be done.

838
00:44:21,970 --> 00:44:27,580
However, there are different
strategies that we could employ,

839
00:44:27,580 --> 00:44:29,080
different engineering
tricks that we

840
00:44:29,080 --> 00:44:34,360
could employ to make our
operation of self-attention

841
00:44:34,360 --> 00:44:37,720
more effective at any
length of document

842
00:44:37,720 --> 00:44:40,810
without having to deal
with this packing problem.

843
00:44:40,810 --> 00:44:46,930
And that comes about by
dynamically changing the context

844
00:44:46,930 --> 00:44:49,810
length from some
maximum value, that's

845
00:44:49,810 --> 00:44:52,570
what you would normally set.

846
00:44:52,570 --> 00:44:55,030
Just use the context
that you have.

847
00:44:55,030 --> 00:44:57,140
But you still have
to create batches

848
00:44:57,140 --> 00:44:58,640
if you want to train
models quickly.

849
00:44:58,640 --> 00:45:00,890
And what that means is that
there's still some padding

850
00:45:00,890 --> 00:45:02,650
if you use this approach.

851
00:45:02,650 --> 00:45:11,230
But you can pad those short
documents to set lengths.

852
00:45:11,230 --> 00:45:16,930
Batch short documents together,
batch long documents together.

853
00:45:16,930 --> 00:45:22,980
This means that we don't need
to pack documents together

854
00:45:22,980 --> 00:45:26,070
to make use of a
long context window.

855
00:45:26,070 --> 00:45:28,930
When a document is long, you
can let its context be long.

856
00:45:28,930 --> 00:45:30,515
When a document
is short, you can

857
00:45:30,515 --> 00:45:31,890
put it with other
short documents

858
00:45:31,890 --> 00:45:35,970
and just use a subset of those
self-attention parameters.

859
00:45:35,970 --> 00:45:38,905
And with traditional
self-attention parameters, keys

860
00:45:38,905 --> 00:45:40,530
and queries, it would
never be a subset

861
00:45:40,530 --> 00:45:44,460
because it's a low dimensional
that, that matrix provides.

862
00:45:44,460 --> 00:45:46,350
With this modified
self-attention,

863
00:45:46,350 --> 00:45:49,050
though, there's a different
shape to the weight matrix.

864
00:45:49,050 --> 00:45:51,210
And that's why it's a
subset of those parameters

865
00:45:51,210 --> 00:45:52,860
that we have to utilize.

866
00:45:52,860 --> 00:45:55,600
And that might be something
worth discussing afterwards.

867
00:45:55,600 --> 00:45:57,270
In other words, how
does the difference

868
00:45:57,270 --> 00:46:00,270
in shapes of
dimensionalities between this

869
00:46:00,270 --> 00:46:04,250
and the standard self-attention
weights shake out?

870
00:46:04,250 --> 00:46:07,750

871
00:46:07,750 --> 00:46:09,970
But we want to get to a
different point for the sake

872
00:46:09,970 --> 00:46:11,300
of this conversation.

873
00:46:11,300 --> 00:46:14,920

874
00:46:14,920 --> 00:46:17,410
What is a model like
this useful for?

875
00:46:17,410 --> 00:46:19,520
That should be a question
that you're asking.

876
00:46:19,520 --> 00:46:21,145
It's a question that
we've been asking.

877
00:46:21,145 --> 00:46:25,780

878
00:46:25,780 --> 00:46:29,620
We're not entirely
certain yet how

879
00:46:29,620 --> 00:46:33,550
an extremely large
model like this

880
00:46:33,550 --> 00:46:36,210
will function on trillions
of tokens, for example.

881
00:46:36,210 --> 00:46:39,700
In other words, can you
expect the same kinds

882
00:46:39,700 --> 00:46:44,230
of outcomes like a
ChatGPT kind of thing

883
00:46:44,230 --> 00:46:46,000
from some of these models?

884
00:46:46,000 --> 00:46:50,440
Human interaction and
RLHF all the rest of that.

885
00:46:50,440 --> 00:46:55,090
Though it's something that
we're considering, but also

886
00:46:55,090 --> 00:46:57,880
at different scales
too, since those

887
00:46:57,880 --> 00:47:01,840
are performant on
their own as well.

888
00:47:01,840 --> 00:47:02,685
But for what?

889
00:47:02,685 --> 00:47:05,430

890
00:47:05,430 --> 00:47:10,470
So the point is, is that
from what we've stress-tested

891
00:47:10,470 --> 00:47:13,890
into the billions, models
can be trained very quickly

892
00:47:13,890 --> 00:47:16,890
on a relatively
small GPU in ways

893
00:47:16,890 --> 00:47:19,960
that we expect when we
cache vector comparisons,

894
00:47:19,960 --> 00:47:22,290
we see really big
speed-ups. When

895
00:47:22,290 --> 00:47:24,820
we don't cache
those comparisons,

896
00:47:24,820 --> 00:47:29,370
you see all of the growth
in computation time

897
00:47:29,370 --> 00:47:31,910
that you would expect from
longer context windows.

898
00:47:31,910 --> 00:47:35,750

899
00:47:35,750 --> 00:47:38,133
This one here,
though, we're trying

900
00:47:38,133 --> 00:47:39,800
to make it really,
really, really small,

901
00:47:39,800 --> 00:47:41,660
the one called potato.

902
00:47:41,660 --> 00:47:43,280
And that's because
we want to see

903
00:47:43,280 --> 00:47:47,150
if we can train a model
from scratch since on very

904
00:47:47,150 --> 00:47:50,990
little data, these models
can fit effectively

905
00:47:50,990 --> 00:47:55,310
with the initializations
that we've developed.

906
00:47:55,310 --> 00:47:58,520
And with the purpose of
starting from scratch,

907
00:47:58,520 --> 00:48:02,030
starting with no data, we're
thinking about edge computing

908
00:48:02,030 --> 00:48:05,480
cases where we could deploy a
